= The window
The menu bar has six menus: VeraBASIC (About), File, Edit, Search, Run and
Help. Their items come in groups between dotted lines, each with its key at
the right (Save as... Ctrl Shift S). Under the bar the title band names the
program and its folder; an asterisk says it has changes not saved. The text
takes the window in 78 columns of 8 x 8 cards, with a scroll bar at the
right: its arrows scroll a line, a click above or below the thumb a page.
The status bar at the bottom shows:

- at the left, the syntax of the keyword at the cursor (PRINT [#h,] [expr
...]), from the keyword table, as you type; a message instead when there is
one (an error, Saved, Not found), until the next key;

- at the right, the state (Ready, Modified, Error or Stopped) and the
cursor's line and column (12:5).

Edit > Line numbers puts a gutter of line numbers at the left.

= Colours
The X16's bitmap has one ink colour, so VeraBASIC colours a program with the
paper of its cards and bold letters:

What: How
keywords (@PRINT, @FOR, @{END IF} ...): bold
strings: on yellow paper
comments (@REM, '): on the sidebar's paper
numbers, names, operators: plain
the line of an error: red paper, the whole line
the line a program was stopped at: the selection's blue
A line's colours come from that line alone: strings and comments end at the
line's end. In dark mode (Preferences) the same colours are the dark
palette's. The Classic theme has no colour map: there the keywords are still
bold, and an error's line is found by the cursor and the status bar.

= Typing
Return starts a new line indented as the one before; after a line that opens
a block (@FOR, @WHILE, @REPEAT, @DO, @IF ... @THEN at the line's end,
@{SELECT CASE}, @CASE, @SUB, @FUNCTION, @TRY, @CATCH, @ELSE, @DIALOG, @MENU)
by two more. A word that closes a block (@NEXT, @WEND, @UNTIL, @LOOP, @{END
IF}, @ELSE, @CASE ...) typed at the inner indentation moves back by two when
you press Return (@{END SELECT} by four). Tab types two blanks.

The keys are Veranda Edit's: the arrows, Home, End, PgUp, PgDn (with Shift
to select, with Ctrl by words and to the text's ends), Backspace, Delete,
Esc (no selection); the mouse places the cursor, a drag selects, a double
click selects a word, the wheel scrolls.

Keys
Ctrl+N, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+Shift+S, Ctrl+Q: New, Open, Save, Save as, Quit
Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+A: Undo, Cut, Copy, Paste, Select all
Ctrl+Shift+F: Format
Ctrl+F, F3, Ctrl+H, Ctrl+L: Find and replace, Find next, Replace and find
next, Go to line
Ctrl+K, F8: Check; the next error
Ctrl+R, Ctrl+C: Run; Stop (while the program runs)
F5: the last program's output again
F9, F6, F7, F10, F11: a breakpoint; Continue, Step, Step over, Step out
(Debugging)
Ctrl+W, F12, Tab: a watch; the trace; the keys to the debugger's pane
F1: help on the keyword at the cursor
Cut, Copy and Paste use the Text Scrap, as Veranda Edit and geoWrite do, so
text moves between them.

= Files
File > Open and Save as are Veranda's Open and Save dialog: the Documents
folder first, folders, and names of up to 63 characters (Counting the birds
at the feeder.bas). A program is saved as plain Latin-1 text with the line
ends it had (LF for a new one), so any PC editor reads it, and vbasic.py
checks and runs it on the PC. Saving writes a new file first and then
replaces the old one.

The example programs are in /DOCUMENTS/VeraBASIC Examples.

= Check, Run, Stop
Check (Run > Check, Ctrl+K) tokenizes and prepares the program on the X16,
as the runtime will before running it: every line, every block, every name.
The first error goes red, the cursor goes to it and the status bar says what
it is (Line 6: NEXT j does not match FOR i% in line 2). F8 (Search > Next
error) checks again and goes to the next error after that line, until No
more errors after line 6.

Run (Ctrl+R) checks the program, then runs it. The editor's screen is kept
in RAM banks and the program gets the screen: a console window for @PRINT
and @INPUT, or its own windows and menus. When the program ends:

- at its @END (or its last line), a console program's output stays on the
screen until you press a key or click; a program of windows goes straight
back;

- stopped by Ctrl+C (or Run > Stop), the editor comes back with the line it
was at in blue (Stopped in line 12);

- stopped by an error, the editor comes back at its line, in red, with the
error in the status bar (Error 11: Division by zero (line 3)) and, whole, in
a pane at the right: the line, the procedure and the statement in canonical
form (Debugging);

- stopped at a breakpoint, a step or @STOP, the program is kept: the editor
shows its line and the debugger's pane, and Continue (F6) sends it on
(Debugging).

The Run menu has the debugger's commands under these: Continue, Step, Step
over, Step out, Breakpoint, Add a watch..., Trace (TRON) and The trace.

The editor's screen comes back as it was. F5 (Run > Output) shows the last
program's console again.

The editor and the program are one application: Alt+Tab puts both aside,
with the program running or not, and brings them back as they were. (With
512 KB of RAM there are not banks enough for that: the switcher offers to
quit VeraBASIC instead. Save first; on a 2 MB machine it goes aside.)

= Format
Edit > Format (Ctrl+Shift+F) rewrites the program in its canonical form:
keywords in upper case, names spelt as where they first appear, one blank
between words and none inside parentheses or before , ; :, literals as you
spelt them, comments and @DATA as they are, each line's indentation kept
(rounded to an even number of blanks). It is what vbasic.py canon gives on
the PC (guarantee G2, docs/vbasic/FORMAT.md). Format needs a program that
checks without errors. Undo takes it back when the program fits the undo
log.

= Help
F1 opens the help at the keyword under the cursor (compound keywords too: F1
on IF after END is END IF); on any other word, the contents. Help >
Contents, Index, Tutorial and Error messages open those pages. In the help
viewer:

Keys
Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn; the scroll bar: scroll
Tab, Shift+Tab: the next link, the one before
Return, a click: follow the link
Backspace: the page before
F1: the contents
Esc: back to the program
The help files are in /VERANDA/HELP: KEYWORDS.HLP (every keyword and
constant: syntax, text, an example; made from the keyword table),
TUTORIAL.HLP (the tutorial), ERRORS.HLP (the error messages) and EDITOR.HLP
(this chapter and the next, Debugging). They are plain text that Veranda
Edit reads too.

= Memory
The editor and the runtime share main RAM: the editor's own part takes the
interpreter's place while you edit, and the two change places when you check
or run. A program run from the editor has 2,436 bytes of main RAM for its
names (about 300 names; under VeraBASIC Run and in an application 3,052).
The editor keeps the text in RAM banks (64 KB at most for a program), two
banks for undo, and while a program runs six banks for its screen when there
are banks to spare; otherwise it draws its window again when you come back.

See also: @Contents

= Debugging
VeraBASIC's debugger is part of the editor: a program run from it can be
stopped at a line, looked at, changed, and sent on, and its screen comes
back as it was. VeraBASIC Run has none of it; a finished program's @TRON
writes to the emulator's log there.

@{Breakpoints}
@{The pane}
@{Watches}
@{Stepping}
@{The program's screen}
@{TRON and the trace}
