= Breakpoints
F9 (Run > Breakpoint) puts a breakpoint on the cursor's line, or takes it
off. The line-number gutter comes on, and the line's card in it turns red
with a . Up to 32 lines can have one. When a run reaches a line with a
breakpoint, the program stops before the line: the editor comes back with
the line on the selection's blue paper and the pane at the right (below). A
breakpoint stops a program each time its line is entered: on a loop's body,
at each pass; on a @FOR, @WHILE or @DO line itself, once, when the loop
starts. The breakpoints stay with their lines while you type above them.

A @STOP statement in the program stops it too, after @STOP, as a breakpoint
would. (In VeraBASIC Run @STOP ends the program: error 1, Stopped.)

  ' a breakpoint on the line of n = n + i stops it ten times; STOP once
  OPTION INTEGER
  n = 0
  FOR i = 1 TO 10
    n = n + i
  NEXT
  STOP
  PRINT "n is"; n

= The pane
While a program is stopped, the text narrows and the pane takes the window's
right 33 columns:

Part: What it shows
the head: Stopped in line 15, why (a breakpoint, a step, @STOP), and the
procedure: SUB Twice, FUNCTION Fact or main
Call stack: the procedures that are running, the innermost first, each with
the line it is at: the line stopped at, then each caller's line (the line of
its call); a @GOSUB too (GOSUB, main); six of them, then ...
Watches: four expressions and their values (below)
Variables: the procedure's own variables first (its parameters, its @LOCALs,
a @FUNCTION's result under its name) on white, then the program's globals on
the pane's colour; each with its name, its type (int, num for a float, str)
and its value; an array as int() and its upper bounds (2, 3)
The values are the program's own, read while it is stopped: a string in
quotes (the first 20 characters), a float as @PRINT writes it.

Tab gives the pane the keys: Up and Down choose a watch or a variable, PgUp
and PgDn page through the variables, and Return does what the row allows:

- on a variable, a box asks for its new value: an expression, as in the
program (99, n 2, "Swift"); the program goes on with it;

- on an array, the pane shows its elements, the last index fastest (grid(1,
2) is element 6 of a grid(2, 3)); Up, Down, PgUp and PgDn move along them,
Esc goes back to the variables;

- on a watch, a box asks for its expression.

Esc or Tab gives the keys back to the text. A click on a row chooses it, a
second click is Return.

= Watches
Ctrl+W (Run > Add a watch...) asks for an expression to watch: up to four,
kept for the next runs too. Each is evaluated at every break, by the
program's own evaluator, so it may use the program's variables (the
procedure's own first), array elements, the operators and the built-in
functions: total 2, squares(3) + 1, LEFT$(name$, 4) + "!", n MOD 3 = 0. A
watch may not call the program's @SUBs and @FUNCTIONs (they would run the
program), and a name the program has not got shows No such variable. Delete
(in the pane) takes a watch off.

= Stepping
Key: The program goes on until
F6: Continue: a breakpoint or @STOP, or its end
F7: Step: the next line it enters, in any procedure
F10: Step over: the next line entered in this procedure or one that called
it: the procedures it calls run through (their breakpoints still stop them)
F11: Step out: the next line entered in a procedure that called this one:
the rest of this one runs through
With no program stopped, F7 runs the program and stops before its first
line; Ctrl+R (Run) while one is stopped continues it, and Run > Stop ends
it. A step goes by lines as the runtime enters them: the statements after :
on a line run with it, and when a loop goes round, the step stops at the
first line of its body. The depth Step over and Step out compare is the
BASIC stack's: its procedures' frames and its @GOSUBs.

  ' F9 on the line of RETURN n * Fact(n - 1), Run: four breaks, the call
  ' stack one FUNCTION Fact deeper each time; F11 then goes back out
  OPTION INTEGER
  FUNCTION Fact(n)
    IF n <= 1 THEN RETURN 1
    RETURN n * Fact(n - 1)
  END FUNCTION
  PRINT "4! ="; Fact(4)

= The program's screen
When a program stops, its screen (its windows, the colours of its cards, its
palette, its sprites, its menu bar) is kept in a RAM bank and on VERA's back
screen, and its timers are held; Continue and the steps give all of it back
as it was (in about 60 ms) and its timers go on. While it is stopped, the
editor's menus and keys are the editor's, and its own handlers do not run.
Gate vb_debug compares a program's screen after a break with the same
program run without one, VERA's bytes and all.

A stopped program keeps its banks. New, Open and Quit wait until it has
ended (Run > Stop); Check and Format too.

= TRON and the trace
@TRON in a program, or Run > Trace (TRON) for the whole of the next runs,
records each line the program enters: its number, or its line in the text
for a line without one. The editor keeps the last 200; F12 (Run > The trace)
shows them in the pane, the newest at the bottom, each with its line's text;
PgUp and PgDn scroll, F12 or Esc closes it. @TROFF stops the recording. The
lines are those vbasic.py run --trace writes for the same program (VB TRON
n), which gate vb_debug checks.

  ' the trace: 5, 6, 7, 6, 7, 6, 7, 8 (lines without numbers: their line
  ' in the text)
  OPTION INTEGER
  TRON
  FOR i = 1 TO 3
    s = s + i
  NEXT
  TROFF
  PRINT s

In VeraBASIC Run, @TRON writes TRON n lines to the emulator's console
(LogText); on the X16 itself they go nowhere.

= Error messages
An error that stops the program is shown in the status bar and, whole, in
the pane at the right: its number and words, the line, the procedure it
happened in, and the statement as Format would write it:

Error 13, Type mismatch, in line 8 (SUB Tick): total = n + name$

An error of a file or a folder has the kernel's own error after its words
(the number @ERRFILE gives):

Error 50, File not found (kernel 5), in line 4: OPEN #1, "No such file.txt",
"r"

F1 on that pane opens the error's help page (Help > Error messages lists
them all); Esc closes it. The console has the shorter form (Error 13, Type
mismatch, in line 8), which @TRY ... @CATCH, @ON @ERROR @GOTO, @ERR, @ERR$
and @ERL see.

VeraBASIC Run says it in its box when an error stops a program, in lines
that fit the box: the program's name, Error 13 in line 8: and Type mismatch
(the console's line may be under the program's windows).

= The debugger's memory
The debugger costs two RAM banks while VeraBASIC is open (its code on the
runtime's side, which also holds the trace, the breakpoints and the values
asked for, and the pane's code), one more while a program runs (its names,
record 0) and one more once it has stopped (its screen's colours, its menu
bar, its names in main RAM). With 512 KB, VeraBASIC holds 31 banks when no
program runs.

See also: @Contents
